092529296875, 0.0184783935546875, -0.039154052734375, 0.0002053976058959961, 0.0183258056640625, -0.006168365478515625, 0.0157318115234375, -0.0132904052734375, 0.0328369140625, 0.049530029296875, 0.032562255859375, 0.006504058837890625, 0.0092620849609375, -0.0239410400390625, 0.01312255859375, 0.053131103515625, -0.041778564453125, -0.013214111328125, -0.05352783203125, -0.052764892578125, 0.0104827880859375, 0.0002663135528564453, -0.005260467529296875, -0.01047515869140625, 0.01230621337890625, -0.007595062255859375, 0.01323699951171875, -0.0166778564453125, -0.019287109375, -0.0237274169921875, -0.004116058349609375, 0.003162384033203125, 0.005313873291015625, -0.00936126708984375, -0.003387451171875, 0.00012445449829101562, 0.028350830078125, 0.0007786750793457031, 0.00807952880859375, 0.03375244140625, -0.002170562744140625, 0.0265960693359375, -0.016998291015625, -0.006198883056640625, 0.0179290771484375, -0.006908416748046875, -0.0026569366455078125, 0.01052093505859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This process requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, implementations based on in-house small language models (SLMs) offer distinct advantages over large language models (LLMs) and third-party solutions. Specifically, in-house SLMs provide lower operational costs, enhanced data security and confidentiality\u2014since sensitive financial data does not leave the organisation and can be protected by internal access controls and encryption\u2014and greater interpretability due to their smaller size and simpler architecture.\n\n**Introduction**  \nWe explore the use of in-house SLMs for invoice categorisation, focusing on two models: a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a compact transformer-based model (DeBERTa). These models were selected due to their strong performance on sentence-level tasks and their suitability for fine-tuning on domain-specific data, which is essential for financial applications where data privacy is critical. By \"compact,\" we refer to models that are smaller and more efficient than typical LLMs, making them practical for in-house deployment. Throughout this study, we aim to clarify technical concepts and provide actionable insights for organisations considering secure, automated financial document processing.\n\n**Methodology**  \nWe first analyse the pre-trained embedding geometry of SBERT and DeBERTa on a proprietary financial invoice corpus. Here, \"embedding geometry\" refers to how the models represent sentences as points in a high-dimensional space; the arrangement and grouping of these points can reveal patterns in the data. For example, invoices from the same vendor may cluster together in this space. We observed that the overall distribution of these embeddings is not uniform in all directions (globally anisotropic\u2014meaning the points are stretched out more in some directions), but within smaller groups, the embeddings are more evenly spread (locally isotropic\u2014meaning the points are distributed similarly in all directions). These local clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity, suggesting that vendor information is a key factor in how the models represent invoice text.\n\nOur dataset consisted of approximately 10,000 labelled invoices, sampled from multiple clients across diverse industries, including retail, manufacturing, and professional services. The invoices covered a wide range of expense types and GL codes, with stratified sampling used to ensure balanced representation of rare categories. Selection criteria included invoice completeness, clarity of GL code assignment, and representation of both frequent and infrequent vendors.\n\nFor fine-tuning, we used supervised learning with cross-entropy loss, training each model on the labelled invoices. We employed the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 2e-5, batch size of 32, and early stopping based on validation loss. Fine-tuning was performed on a single GPU. To further protect sensitive data, all processing was conducted within a secure, access-controlled environment, and data was encrypted at rest and in transit.\n\nIn summary, this methodology allowed us to evaluate SLM performance in a realistic, privacy-preserving setting, while also enabling analysis of how model representations relate to invoice characteristics.\n\n**Results**  \nAfter fine-tuning, SBERT achieved an accuracy of 0.96 on invoice classification, outperforming both a zero-shot LLM and a baseline using only vendor identity. Notably, SBERT showed improved performance for smaller, challenging categories (e.g., niche expense types with fewer than 50 examples) and for new clients. For example, in case studies involving three recently onboarded businesses\u2014each with unique vendor mixes and limited historical data\u2014SBERT maintained high accuracy and correctly classified invoices in categories that had previously been misclassified by rule-based systems. For the generalisation task\u2014predicting correct GL codes for clients with roughly 100 client-specific invoices\u2014SBERT achieved an F1 score of 0.9, indicating a strong balance between precision and recall. These results demonstrate the model\u2019s reliability for practical invoice categorisation, even when only a small amount of client data is available.\n\n**Geometric Analysis and Interpretability**  \nOur geometric analysis, which involved visualising and quantifying the clustering of embeddings, revealed that the structure of the pre-trained embedding space is associated with classification performance. To clarify, \"anisotropic\" means that the embeddings are spread out more in some directions than others, while \"isotropic\" clusters are more evenly distributed in all directions. This clustering, especially by vendor, suggests that the model leverages vendor-specific language patterns to inform its predictions. Interestingly, when we provided structured input (such as tabular or explicitly segmented invoice data) that would typically aid a human reader, the SLM\u2019s performance did not improve. This may be because the model\u2019s internal representation already captures the relevant structure from unstructured text, or because the model is optimised to process natural language rather than structured formats. This highlights a difference between human and model interpretability and suggests that further structuring of input data may not always benefit SLM performance. For practical applications, this finding implies that organisations may not need to invest in additional data structuring for SLM-based automation, but future research could explore whether hybrid approaches or interpretability tools can further enhance trust and transparency in model decisions.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nIn summary, our findings demonstrate that in-house SLMs like SBERT and DeBERTa are effective for invoice categorisation, offering high accuracy, strong generalisation to new clients and rare categories, and enhanced data security through internal deployment, access controls, and encryption. The embedding geometry analysis provides insight into model behaviour, and our results suggest that further structuring of input data may not always benefit SLM performance. Future research could, for example, investigate hybrid models that combine structured and unstructured data, develop visual analytics tools to make embedding spaces more interpretable to finance professionals, or conduct longitudinal studies on model adaptation to evolving invoice formats. This approach is promising for organisations seeking secure, interpretable, and cost-effective automation of financial document processing."}}